<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Love song · c. 1850 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Scarborough Fair</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English folk song in which a singer asks a lost love to do impossible tasks. It is sung to a tune collected from a lead miner in 1947.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Folk singers of northern England (Yorkshire and Northumbria)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Yorkshire and Northumbria, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>19th century (as &quot;Scarborough Fair&quot;); the related ballad &quot;The Elfin Knight&quot; is traced to 1670</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics traditional (Kidson's text published 1891))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Scarborough Fair&quot; (Roud 12, Child 2) is a traditional ballad of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its words have much in common with the Scottish ballad &quot;The Elfin Knight&quot;, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-james-child">Francis James Child</a> collected as his second ballad and which has been traced back to 1670; in it an elf threatens to carry off a young woman unless she performs an impossible task, and she answers with impossible tasks of her own. Dozens of versions existed by the end of the 18th century. Many name other places, such as Wittingham Fair or Cape Ann, or no place at all, under titles like &quot;The Lovers' Tasks&quot; or &quot;My Father Gave Me an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> of Land&quot;. The mention of the fair at Scarborough and the herb refrain belong to 19th-century versions, and may have been borrowed from &quot;Riddles Wisely Expounded&quot; (Child 1). The herb refrain may also have grown out of an older refrain by mishearing.</p><p>The Scarborough or Whittingham Fair form was most common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and Northumbria, where it was sung to several tunes, often in the Dorian mode. Frank Kidson printed a version in Traditional Tunes (1891), saying he had collected it at Whitby; his words are the best-known text. The tune best known today came from Mark Anderson (1874–1953), a retired lead miner of Middleton-in-Teesdale in County Durham, who sang it to Ewan MacColl in 1947. No recording of Anderson's version was made. MacColl printed it in a book of Teesdale folk songs and recorded it with Peggy Seeger. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lomax">John Lomax</a> recorded a related version, &quot;The Cambric Shirt&quot;, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> Ann Griffin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1939, and other field recordings followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> (1958), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> (1966) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> (1976).</p><p>The first commercial recording was by Gordon Heath and Lee Payant, Americans who ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> nightclub, on an album of 1955; A. L. Lloyd and MacColl recorded it with Kidson's tune in 1956, and Audrey Coppard was the first to record Anderson's tune the same year. In the English folk revival Martin Carthy learned it from MacColl and Seeger's songbook and recorded it on his debut album in 1965. Before that, Bob Dylan had borrowed the melody and some lines from Carthy's arrangement for &quot;Girl from the North Country&quot; (1963).</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1965 Paul Simon learned the song from Carthy. Simon &amp; Garfunkel set it in counterpoint with &quot;Canticle&quot;, a reworking of Simon's anti-war song &quot;The Side of a Hill&quot;, and released &quot;Scarborough Fair/Canticle&quot; in 1966; it reached a wider public on the soundtrack of The Graduate in 1968. The copyright named only Simon and Garfunkel. Carthy felt the traditional source should have been credited, and although the duo later paid his publisher a settlement, Carthy himself received nothing. The rift lasted until Simon and Carthy sang it together at the Hammersmith <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> in October 2000. The ballad has since been recorded by singers and bands from folk to metal, and used in films, television and a video game.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A singer sends a message to a former lover at the fair: if she will perform a list of impossible tasks, such as making a shirt without a seam, he will take her back. In answer, she sets him impossible tasks of her own. Beneath the riddling play the song speaks of love that has been lost and cannot be won back.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>—The male:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O, where are you going? To Scarborough Fair?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Remember me to a lass that lives there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For she was once a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And tell her to make me a cambric shirt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Without any seam or needlework,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And then she shall be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And tell her to wash it in yonder dry well,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where no water sprung nor a drop of rain fell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And then she shall be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And tell her to dry it on yonder thorn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Which never bore blossom since Adam was born,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And then she shall be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>—The female:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O, will you find me an acre of land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Between the sea foam and the sea sand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or never you'll be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O, will you plough it with a ram's horn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And sow it all over with one peppercorn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or else you'll be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or will you reap it with a sickle of leather,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And tie it all up with a peacock's feather,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Or never you'll be a true love of mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And when you have done and have finished your work,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Savoury, sage, rosemary and thyme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You can come to me for your cambric shirt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And then you shall be a true love of mine.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; the words published by Frank Kidson (1891), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarborough_Fair_(ballad)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
